    <h1 class="article-title">Business Intelligence Reporting: How to Build a Monthly Report Leaders Actually Use</h1>
    <p class="article-dek">How to build a short, trusted monthly report with the right KPIs, a repeatable structure, clear commentary and Power BI automation that leaders actually use.</p>

    <div class="article-byline">
      <div class="article-avatar" style="background:#E9584C;">MG</div>
      <div><div class="article-author">Megan</div><div class="article-role">Growth Lead, Tycoon Clap</div></div>
      <div class="article-time">10 min read</div>
    </div>

    <div class="article-hero-art"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 160" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="28" y="20" width="184" height="120" rx="14" fill="#FDEED0"/><rect x="58" y="38" width="100" height="84" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#2E3A55" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M72 58h50M72 72h72" stroke="#2E3A55" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round"/><rect x="72" y="90" width="14" height="22" fill="#4C9C8B"/><rect x="94" y="82" width="14" height="30" fill="#E9584C"/><rect x="116" y="74" width="14" height="38" fill="#F3B94D"/><circle cx="178" cy="100" r="18" fill="#4C9C8B"/><path d="M170 100l6 6 10-12" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></div>

    <div class="article-grid">
      <article class="article-content" id="articleContent">
        <div class="qa-box"><span class="qa-label">Quick answer</span><p>A monthly <strong>business intelligence report</strong> that leaders use is <strong>short, consistent and decision-focused</strong>. Lead with a one-page summary, track <strong>5 to 10 KPIs</strong> against target and last period, add two or three lines of commentary on what changed and why, and finish with clear actions and owners. Automate the data with a tool such as <strong>Power BI</strong> so the team spends time on insight, not assembling numbers.</p></div>
        <p class="updated-note">Last updated November 2026 · General guidance for US small and mid-size businesses</p>

        <p>Most companies have reports. Far fewer have reports that anyone reads. The classic pattern: a 30-page deck is assembled by hand over several days, emailed on the 10th, skimmed by two people and filed. Meanwhile, decisions are made on gut feel. Good <strong>business intelligence reporting</strong> reverses that. It is brief, trusted, delivered on time and tied to decisions. This guide shows how to build a monthly management report that earns its place on the calendar.</p>
        <p>New to the topic? Our <a href="what-is-business-intelligence-guide.html">business intelligence guide</a> covers the basics, and our <a href="how-to-build-a-business-dashboard-in-power-bi.html">Power BI dashboard tutorial</a> covers the build.</p>

        <h2 id="why-ignored">Why most reports get ignored</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Too long.</strong> Readers cannot find the point, so they stop looking.</li>
          <li><strong>No story.</strong> Numbers are listed without saying what they mean.</li>
          <li><strong>Mistrust.</strong> If two reports show different revenue, people stop believing both.</li>
          <li><strong>Lateness.</strong> By the time it arrives, the moment for action has passed.</li>
          <li><strong>No owner or action.</strong> Nothing happens because no one is responsible for the next step.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="start-decisions">Step 1: Start with decisions, not data</h2>
        <p>Before choosing a single chart, ask each leader: "What decisions do you make every month, and what do you need to see to make them well?" A sales leader may decide on hiring and territory; a finance lead on cash and spend; an operations lead on capacity and delays. Each metric in the report should map to one of these decisions. If you cannot name the decision, leave the metric out.</p>

        <h2 id="kpis">Step 2: Pick a short list of KPIs</h2>
        <p>Choose a small number of headline KPIs and define each in writing: the formula, the data source and the owner. Examples by function:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Revenue and sales:</strong> revenue vs target, new customers, average order or deal value, pipeline coverage, win rate.</li>
          <li><strong>Marketing:</strong> leads, cost per lead, conversion rate, cost to acquire a customer, return on ad spend.</li>
          <li><strong>Finance:</strong> gross margin, operating expenses vs budget, cash position, receivables outstanding.</li>
          <li><strong>Operations:</strong> on-time delivery, utilization, backlog, defect or return rate.</li>
          <li><strong>Customers:</strong> retention or churn, satisfaction score, support response time.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>For small businesses, five to ten headline KPIs is plenty. More detail can live one click deeper. See our <a href="power-bi-consulting-for-small-business.html">small-business Power BI guide</a> for practical KPI examples.</p>
        <div class="article-pull">"If everything is a priority, nothing is. Pick the numbers that change decisions."</div>

        <h2 id="structure">Step 3: Use a repeatable structure</h2>
        <p>A consistent layout lets readers find the same thing in the same place every month. A proven <strong>monthly management report template</strong> has five parts:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Headline summary (page 1):</strong> three to five sentences on how the month went, plus the top KPIs with traffic-light status against target.</li>
          <li><strong>KPI scorecard:</strong> each KPI with this month, last month, same month last year and target.</li>
          <li><strong>What changed and why:</strong> two or three bullet points of commentary for the biggest movements, written by someone who knows the context.</li>
          <li><strong>Risks and issues:</strong> what could derail the next month, with an owner and date.</li>
          <li><strong>Actions and decisions:</strong> what we will do, who owns it and when. Review last month's actions first.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Put detailed tables, regional breakdowns and data definitions in an appendix or a drill-through dashboard.</p>

        <h2 id="examples">Business intelligence report examples</h2>
        <h3>Small e-commerce brand</h3>
        <p>One page: revenue vs target, orders, average order value, conversion rate, ad spend and return, repeat purchase rate, inventory days on hand. Commentary: "Conversion dropped after the site update on the 12th; fix scheduled." Action: pause two campaigns with weak returns.</p>
        <h3>Professional services firm</h3>
        <p>One page: billable utilization, revenue vs forecast, pipeline coverage, receivables aging, new and lost clients. Commentary: "Utilization is below target because two projects started late." Action: schedule pipeline review for two stalled deals.</p>
        <h3>SaaS company</h3>
        <p>One page: monthly recurring revenue, net new customers, churn, expansion, support backlog, product usage. Commentary: "Churn rose among customers who skipped onboarding." Action: add an onboarding check-in call.</p>

        <h2 id="design">Step 4: Design for fast reading</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Lead with the answer: the headline first, supporting data after.</li>
          <li>Use charts that show a trend or comparison clearly, and avoid decoration.</li>
          <li>Use consistent colors, and use red and green sparingly so they keep their meaning.</li>
          <li>Label every metric with its period and unit.</li>
          <li>Keep the main view to one page or one screen.</li>
          <li>Write commentary in plain language. "Revenue is 8% below plan" beats "Variance observed in top-line performance."</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="automation">Step 5: Automate with Power BI</h2>
        <p><strong>Power BI reporting</strong> removes the manual assembly. A typical setup connects your accounting, CRM, e-commerce and advertising tools to a single data model, refreshes on a schedule and publishes a report to the Power BI service. From there you can share dashboards with colleagues, and, depending on your licensing, send email subscriptions or export a snapshot for the monthly meeting. Automation gives you three benefits: the numbers are ready on day one of the month, they match across the company, and your analyst's time goes to commentary instead of copy-paste. Our guide to the <a href="power-bi-service-vs-power-platform.html">Power BI service and Power Platform</a> explains how sharing and automation fit together.</p>

        <h2 id="trust">Step 6: Make the numbers trustworthy</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One definition per metric.</strong> Publish a short data dictionary.</li>
          <li><strong>One source of truth.</strong> Pull from the system of record, not a spreadsheet copy.</li>
          <li><strong>Reconcile before release.</strong> Check key totals, such as revenue, against finance.</li>
          <li><strong>Show data freshness.</strong> Display "updated on" so readers know how current the numbers are.</li>
          <li><strong>Log changes.</strong> If a definition changes, say so in the report.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="meeting">Step 7: Make the report part of a meeting</h2>
        <p>A report only changes behavior when it is discussed. Hold a 30 to 45 minute monthly review where leaders read the summary, challenge the commentary and agree actions. Send the report one day before so the meeting is for decisions, not reading. Begin each meeting by reviewing last month's actions. This simple habit turns reporting into management.</p>

        <h2 id="review">Review and improve the report every quarter</h2>
        <p>A report is a product, and products need maintenance. Each quarter, ask readers three questions: which section did you use, which did you ignore, and what decision did you wish the report had supported? Remove anything unused, add what is missing and update targets as the business changes. Check that data definitions still match how the company works, especially after a new product line, pricing change or system migration. Teams that treat reporting as a living process keep it useful, while teams that never revisit it end up with the same bloated deck they started with.</p>

        <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Adding a metric every time someone asks and never removing any.</li>
          <li>Reporting activity (emails sent) instead of outcomes (meetings booked).</li>
          <li>Letting each department define revenue its own way.</li>
          <li>Building a beautiful dashboard with no commentary or actions.</li>
          <li>Treating the report as finished instead of reviewing it every quarter.</li>
          <li>Making the analyst a human export button instead of an advisor.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="who-builds">Who should build and own it?</h2>
        <p>Ownership matters. A BI-focused analyst or consultant builds the model and dashboards, while a business leader owns the commentary and the actions. If you are unsure who should do what, read our guides on <a href="business-intelligence-analyst-vs-data-analyst.html">BI analyst vs data analyst</a> and <a href="outsource-business-intelligence-vs-hire-analyst-cost-comparison.html">outsourcing BI vs hiring an analyst</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="our-approach">How Tycoon Clap helps</h2>
        <p>We design monthly reporting around your decisions: agree the KPIs, connect your data, build the Power BI report, automate the refresh and train your team to write the commentary. If your current reports take days to build and nobody reads them, book a strategy call and bring an example.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
          <details open><summary>What is business intelligence reporting?</summary><p class="faq-a">Business intelligence reporting is the regular delivery of data-driven reports and dashboards that show how the business is performing against its goals. It turns raw data from different systems into consistent, trusted metrics that leaders use to make decisions.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What should a monthly management report include?</summary><p class="faq-a">A one-page summary of headline results, a small set of KPIs with comparison to target and prior period, short commentary explaining the changes, risks and open issues, and clear actions with owners. Supporting detail goes in an appendix or drill-down view.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How many KPIs should a monthly report have?</summary><p class="faq-a">Fewer than most teams think. A common guideline is five to ten headline KPIs for leadership, with department views holding more detail. If everything is a priority, nothing is.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What is the difference between a dashboard and a report?</summary><p class="faq-a">A dashboard is interactive and lets people explore current data. A report is a structured, time-stamped summary, often with commentary, that captures a month or period for review and decisions. Many companies use both: the dashboard for exploration, the report for the monthly meeting.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can Power BI produce monthly reports?</summary><p class="faq-a">Yes. Power BI can connect to your data sources, refresh on a schedule, and deliver reports to colleagues through the Power BI service, including email subscriptions and exports. Features available depend on your licensing, so check your plan.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How do I get leaders to actually read the report?</summary><p class="faq-a">Keep it short, lead with the answer, tie every metric to a decision, and discuss it in a regular meeting instead of just emailing it. Remove sections nobody uses, and ask leaders what they would change after each cycle.</p></details>
        </div><p style="font-size:.88rem;color:var(--ink-dim);line-height:1.7;margin-top:2rem;">Examples are illustrative and not client data. Power BI features and sharing options depend on your licensing and Microsoft updates; check Microsoft Learn for current capabilities.</p>
